**Proof.**

**Substitution.** Let $a = 1/x,\ b = 1/y,\ c = 1/z$. The constraint $xy+yz+zx = xyz$ becomes
$$a + b + c = 1, \quad a,b,c > 0.$$
Set $u = ab+bc+ca$ and $v = abc$. Then:
$$x+y+z = \frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{c} = \frac{u}{v}, \qquad xy+yz+zx = \frac{1}{ab}+\frac{1}{bc}+\frac{1}{ca} = \frac{a+b+c}{abc} = \frac{1}{v}.$$

The inequality becomes $\dfrac{u^2}{v^2} - 81 \geq k\!\left(\dfrac{1}{v}-27\right)$, i.e. (multiplying by $v^2>0$):
$$u^2 - 81v^2 \geq kv(1-27v). \tag{$\star$}$$

**Sign analysis.** By AM-GM, $v = abc \le \left(\frac{a+b+c}{3}\right)^3 = \frac{1}{27}$, so $1-27v \ge 0$. By Cauchy-Schwarz, $(x+y+z)\!\left(\frac1x+\frac1y+\frac1z\right) \ge 9$, giving $x+y+z \ge 9$, hence $u^2/v^2 \ge 81$, i.e. $u^2 - 81v^2 \ge 0$. Both sides of $(\star)$ are nonneg, so we seek
$$k = \inf_{(a,b,c)} \frac{u^2 - 81v^2}{v(1-27v)}.$$

**Claim:** $k = \dfrac{15}{4}$, i.e. $4u^2 + 81v^2 - 15v \ge 0$ for all $a+b+c=1$, $a,b,c\ge 0$.

